WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.215
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:09.390
KERRY MUNZ: Hi, everybody.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.400
My name is Kerry Munz,
and I'm the director

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.320
of engineering for the
platform infrastructure

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.060
teams at HubSpot.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.800
And I'm here to
tell you the tale

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.130
of going both multi-cloud and
multi-region for the first time

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.620
at HubSpot in Q1 of this year.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.800
There are a few key lessons we
learned from this experience.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.720
And the one that I
want you all to know

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.140
is that it can be done fast.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.850
And the key to this is
treating your infrastructure

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.700
as dynamic as your
application code.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.110
Your infrastructure is code.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.670
So let's start briefly
with what is HubSpot?

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.180
HubSpot is the leading CRM
marketing sales and customer

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.770
experience platform.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.410
We offer sales and marketing
automation software,

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:54.040
abd it helps companies attract
visitors, convert leads, close,

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.200
and delight customers.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.800
We focus on small and
medium businesses.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.020
We offer sale-- excuse me.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.530
We help the small guys nurture
their customer relationships

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.680
as effectively as the big guys.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.470
So this is where our journey
starts, with our CEO.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.250
Brian Halligan is HubSpot CEO.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.020
We were opening an
office in Berlin

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.980
to strengthen our commitment
to our EU customers.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.040
And we needed to give
them better performance,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.490
stronger security.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.500
And Brian wanted
tighter integrations

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.180
with AdWords and G Suite.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.330
And on top of all
that, he wanted

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.450
to offer more free
services to our customers.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.960
As you all may know,
freemium is not free to host.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.580
So we manage our infrastructure
costs very efficiently.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.560
The million-dollar question
with any project is time.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.180
We already had a
lot on our plate

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.780
with a big MySQL migration,
and we were a big migration

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.250
into Kubernetes.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.080
And this added a ton more.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.320
But we knew it was
important to our customers

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.840
and wanted to
support the business.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.180
He told us this would be in Q1.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.730
And he had come to us
in summer of last year.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.410
So the time line was very short.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:24.890
When he said Q1, I
imagined a fiery hellscape.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.780
What I wanted to say
was, go F yourself.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.840
But what I actually
said was, we got this.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.270
So I went to our
infrastructure teams.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.010
I told them that
we are on the hook

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.230
to support our EU customers.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.150
They came up with a few options.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.420
They said we could consider
expanding with AWS.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.390
We could consider spinning
up our own data center.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.890
Or we could consider
a new cloud provider.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.130
So in the end, we went
with Google Cloud.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:58.610
So the rest of this tale is to
show you why that was not scary

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.640
with our foundation of tooling,
how we tested our biggest

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.310
fears fast, and
how we uncovered 11

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.340
years of unknown
dependencies lurking

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:10.490
in our infrastructure to
successfully ship a new EU data

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.490
center in Q1.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.990
So HubSpot's best-kept secret
is our amazing platform

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:19.460
infrastructure teams.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.240
These 14 teams have the mission
to make our 60 other product

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.120
teams as effective and
efficient as possible,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.090
basically to give
them superpowers,

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.240
to improve the reliability and
efficiency of our platform,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.300
and to keep the
free in freemium.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.120
The foundation of all of this
is that everything at HubSpot

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:39.680
is code.

00:03:43.037 --> 00:03:44.620
Have you all ever
heard the expression

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.470
of pets versus cattle
referring to your servers?

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.420
So pets are the
ones that you baby.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.740
You take care of them because
they are mission critical

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.720
and they are replaceable.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.320
So they can destroy your
business if they die.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.830
Cattle are all identical.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.010
You can shoot one in the
head, and another one

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:01.420
will take its place.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.321
At HubSpot, we only have cattle.

00:04:03.321 --> 00:04:03.820
No pets.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:09.860
So our platform supports tooling
for our marketing, sales,

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:11.510
and services customers.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.330
This runs on top of a giant
automation and analytics

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.839
platform that is
processing a ton of data--

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:22.340
data based on analytics,
ads, contacts, email, chat,

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.820
all sorts of things-- processing
data from all sorts of sources

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.750
on behalf of our customers.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.230
So how do we manage
all of that big data?

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.110
There are many different
types of workflows

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.780
that are needed to make it
all work seamlessly together.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.850
We have focused
largely on open source

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.740
because years ago the
cloud was not so mature,

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.860
and we needed the control
to be able to dig in

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.560
and tune our data
services as we needed.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.489
We wanted to be able
to touch the code.

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:51.030
It was also because
of the need to be

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:55.260
able to control costs
to keep freemium free.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.530
We actually have hundreds of
thousands of free customers,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.650
so we run our infrastructure
with costs in mind.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.830
And over the years we wrote
a lot of automation software

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:09.000
to keep our data infrastructure
running smoothly.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.429
Since scale can mean many things
to different companies here,

00:05:12.429 --> 00:05:13.970
I want to give you
some quick numbers

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.386
to get a sense of what it meant
when we said we were going

00:05:16.386 --> 00:05:19.430
multi-cloud and multi-region.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.490
So HBase is the heart
of HubSpot data.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:25.100
Our HBase clusters
are processing up

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.580
to 90 million
requests per second

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:32.600
and processing nearly
half a petabyte a day.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.300
Kafka is HubSpot's bloodstream.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.580
With Kafka, we are handling
around a million requests

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.690
per second.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.670
Elasticsearch is
our nerve system,

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:46.730
supporting nearly half
a billion documents.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.380
The bane of my
existence is MySQL.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.760
We actually have more
than 500 MySQL clusters.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.492
And at HubSpot, they
grow like mushrooms.

00:05:54.492 --> 00:05:56.450
I think our engineers
can't tell the difference

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.360
between a database and a table.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.010
So that totals more than
1,300 individual VMs.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:07.220
And we have historically had
one person managing all of them.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.710
He knew them all personally.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:12.290
Essentially, he had 500 pets.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.540
And there were far too
many manual operations

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.780
to tend to them all.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:19.600
So early last year,
we decided to change

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.410
all of that, which
was lucky, considering

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.080
the move we were going to
need to the EU data center

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:26.890
later in the year.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.680
We needed to orchestrate
the life cycles of those 500

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:34.400
clusters.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.070
And at the same time, a lot
of our relational-style data

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:38.440
was too big.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.130
And we needed to move
a lot of that data

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:45.450
into HBase to be able to
support the request load.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:49.760
We chose Vitess and Kubernetes
to solve this problem for us.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.860
Vitess is an open
source clustering system

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.910
to automate horizontal
scaling of MySQL,

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.470
and it was created
by the YouTube team.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.740
We've become very active
contributors to this project.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.500
It essentially is a
proxy to SQL queries.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.990
It makes sharding very simple
to support horizontal scaling

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:09.420
in an easy way.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.910
It also replaced our
manual and Ansible

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.710
plays that we use
to handle things

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:17.720
like master slave failovers
when machines would fail.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.360
We run Vitess inside
of Kubernetes.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.370
We have years of
experience with Mesos

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:26.120
for orchestrating docker
containers and JVMs for running

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.290
our own applications.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.240
And we were excited
to find a system

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.690
to orchestrate our data stores
as easily as our applications.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:37.160
And we could share server
resources for those 1,300 VMs,

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.500
instead of relying on
dedicated machines.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.070
So our Kubernetes migration
was already under way

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.570
when our CEO came to me.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:48.960
So why did we consider Google?

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.760
We've been running HubSpot
in AWS for 10 years.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.790
When Brian came to us and
said we needed to do better

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:02.290
by our EU customers and we
needed better integrations

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.340
with AdWords and
G Suite, we said

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.791
it was time to survey
the cloud landscape.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:12.440
So BigTable is the
basis for HBase.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.390
We've admired BigTable
performance numbers

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:16.370
for a long time.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:18.920
We put a lot of
resources into HBase

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.410
and dreamed of a future
where Google would take care

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.780
of all those headaches for us.

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:26.270
Plus, if you've ever had a
visit from the Google team

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:28.610
or talked to the
BigTable engineers,

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:30.590
they will walk into
your conference room,

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.150
draw a picture
that looks exactly

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:35.659
like this on your whiteboard,
drop the microphone,

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:37.460
and just leave the room.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:41.260
So we were interested
in knowing more.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:43.100
We were also
interested in Google

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.930
because we are big fans
of their open source.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.406
We have our own
internal commitment

00:08:47.406 --> 00:08:49.280
to open source and have
published some tools,

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:51.654
like Blazar and Singularity,
I'll tell you about a little

00:08:51.654 --> 00:08:52.372
later.

00:08:52.372 --> 00:08:54.080
And we were already
actively contributing

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.710
to Vitess and Kubernetes.

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.600
Our infrastructure team
was also interested

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.690
in seeing what cool things they
could do using Google's network

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:03.800
offerings.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.170
Plus, the timing was perfect.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.815
Google was coming out with a
data center in Frankfurt in Q4.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.720
So the big reason I
am here is to tell

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.480
this tale of the
foundation of our tools

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:19.960
and how that helped us
to move very quickly.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.920
I want to point out
to you a few areas

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:24.380
that we needed to update to
be able to support a new cloud

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:26.450
provider and a new
region, and how

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:28.782
this could work for you too.

00:09:28.782 --> 00:09:30.240
So first I'll give
you some details

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:31.620
about the components
that make up

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.820
our platform so you understand
what I'm talking about.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.540
So right now, we are
running more than 3,000 VMs

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:39.680
in the cloud.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.230
All of our infrastructure
tools interface

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:45.780
with our cloud providers
through a tool called Rainmaker.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.710
Rainmaker is the foundation
of our infrastructure-as-code

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:50.090
approach.

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.220
It wraps all of the
cloud primitive,

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:56.780
like the API's a spin up, spin
up and down VMs, attach disks,

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:58.700
manage storage
buckets, do security

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:01.780
group management,
things like that.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.600
We use Puppet to keep
the configuration for all

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.400
of our VMs consistent.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.980
Puppet updates are
managed through GitHub.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.620
And they're deployed like any
other app in our deployment

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.540
pipeline.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.060
So the HubSpot
product is actually

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:18.910
made up of more than
6,000 microservices, jobs,

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:22.660
and tasks, all running on
top of Singularity in Mesos.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:26.100
Singularity is our open source
app orchestration framework

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.540
that is powered by Apache Mesos.

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:31.810
We introduced Singularity into
our platform a few years ago

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:35.350
to remove the complexity of
running and deploying workloads

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.480
directly to machines.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:40.450
It used to be that engineers
had to worry about everything

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:42.190
for their services and tasks.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:43.660
They would worry
about horizontally

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:45.220
scaling those services.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:48.400
They would worry about whether
the underlying hardware

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:49.590
was failing.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:51.550
So Singularity let
engineers focus

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.740
on what they do best,
which is writing software,

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.435
instead of managing
infrastructure.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:01.600
On top of our app
orchestration framework,

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:03.940
we have a highly
efficient CI/CD pipeline

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:06.550
to make it extremely
easy for our engineers

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:08.110
to build and deploy code fast.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:15.490
We have over 2,000
active GitHub repos

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.760
for all of those
microservices and tasks.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.710
We have actually archived
over 6,000 other repos

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:25.330
over the years, which is because
we are constantly experimenting

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:29.390
and improving how HubSpot runs.

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:34.360
So we wrote Blazar, our
build orchestration tool.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:37.450
We used to run all of our
builds on Jenkins, but because

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:40.810
of the explosive growth
of our repos and branches,

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:42.820
it became too complex to manage.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:46.630
The UI basically turned into
a hellscape for our engineers.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.610
We addressed those
pains by building

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:51.060
Blazar around GitHub webhooks.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.930
It automatically
detects new repos

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:54.850
and requires no
extra configuration

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:57.090
to know exactly what to build.

00:11:57.090 --> 00:12:00.130
Blazar runs inside of
Singularity itself.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:03.579
So last year, Blazar did more
than 2 and 1/2 million builds.

00:12:03.579 --> 00:12:05.620
And this year, we are on
track to run more than 4

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.290
million builds.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:11.350
We also wrote our own
deploy tool, called Orion.

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:13.150
Orion makes it simple
for our engineers

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:16.000
to deploy workloads
into Singularity

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.710
more than 10,000 times a week.

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:21.250
Last year, we in fact
deployed over 900,000 times

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:23.000
to both QA and prod.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.500
And we put a heavy investment
into the user experience

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.350
for Orion because this tool is
so critical for our engineers

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:30.595
to get their work done.

00:12:30.595 --> 00:12:35.960
It supports health checks,
acceptance tests, for example.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.870
We also acknowledge
that engineers do not

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:38.920
write perfect code.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:42.040
So we prioritize
mean time to repair,

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:45.920
making it simple to roll
back changes as well.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:49.760
So now, back to our story
on how we expanded to the EU

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:50.260
with Google.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.350
So to start a project like
this, an important question

00:12:56.350 --> 00:13:01.480
any engineering leader's toolbox
is how can we keep it simple?

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.240
Since HubSpot is made up of
thousands of microservices

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.440
and jobs, making a fully
sharded version of HubSpot

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:11.020
in such a short amount of
time would be reckless.

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:12.880
And we were not reckless.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:15.580
We were confident,
but not reckless.

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:17.980
So we started by
narrowing the focus.

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:20.230
We asked ourselves what was
the most important thing

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:24.520
for reliability and performance
for our customers in the EU.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:27.190
We already have
approximately 70,000 websites

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.830
fronted by Cloudflare across
the globe for fast performance.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.120
But any time data is submitted
by our customers' customers,

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:36.150
it goes back to our
origin in Virginia.

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:38.650
And if there are issues with
the network or any other issues

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:41.152
back at the origin,
that data could be lost.

00:13:41.152 --> 00:13:42.610
So it was important
that we did not

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:46.060
miss collecting any usage data.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:49.140
So we focused on analytics,
click tracking, CTAs,

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:51.230
and those sorts of
collection services.

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:55.730
So as I said
before, we have been

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:58.320
AWS customers for 10 years.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:00.560
So the next most important
thing to figure out

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.620
was what did we
fear about Google?

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.200
So we made a list.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:10.510
And there were, in
fact, three things.

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:13.420
Simply, could we deploy to GCP?

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:16.300
Would the network connection
between AWS and GCP

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:17.770
be fast enough?

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:19.900
And there was this thing
the Google team told us

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.550
about called live migrations.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.210
So to start, the question was
could we simply deploy services

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:29.389
to GCP?

00:14:29.389 --> 00:14:31.180
We wanted to make sure
our engineers didn't

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:34.450
need to care about the
details of running on Google

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:38.790
and also about running
in another region.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:42.660
I mentioned Rainmaker earlier
as our VM management tool.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:46.090
Back in 2012, VM management
was really hard to do.

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:48.780
So we had created
Rainmaker to abstract away

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:53.040
AWS console and primitives and
create one simple way to spin

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:54.960
up servers correctly.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.050
So the thing that we created
six years ago to work better

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.690
with AWS became a key
factor in making it

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:04.070
easy to work with Google too.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:05.930
So what we did was
we looked at the API

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:09.230
calls we needed to make Google
so we could start and stop

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:12.000
machines, set up disks,
configuring networking.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.210
And this is just a matter
of refactoring our code

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:16.370
to create a second adapter.

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:20.000
The first adapter was to handle
the AWS primitives, and then

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:24.080
a second adapter for
Google primitives.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:27.570
Rainmaker APIs all
stayed exactly the same.

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:31.330
So all of our automated
tooling stayed the same.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:36.176
Then we updated the Rainmaker
UI, added Belgium as an option

00:15:36.176 --> 00:15:37.550
because the
Frankfurt data center

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:42.340
didn't exist at that time,
and bam, we had Google.

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:44.480
Our second fear was
the network connection

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.750
between AWS and Google,
since our data is now going

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.040
to be crossing the Atlantic.

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:54.950
So we used the standard
cloud provider VPNs

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.590
to connect the two networks
together across the ocean,

00:15:57.590 --> 00:15:59.870
and it was giving us
approximately 1 gigabit

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:05.370
per second and taking time
to ramp up to that speed.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:09.402
To support HBase replication
and our Kafka streams,

00:16:09.402 --> 00:16:10.860
we wanted to make
sure we could get

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:14.680
10 gigabits per second
throughput across the ocean.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.640
And we wanted to treat our
European data center just

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.725
like any other availability
zone within Amazon.

00:16:21.725 --> 00:16:23.100
So the way that
our team did that

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:24.750
was actually rather clever.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:29.100
They noticed that they would
get 6 milliseconds of latency

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:31.440
between our data
center in Virginia

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:34.660
and Google's data
center in Virginia.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:38.460
So we set up strongSwan, which
is an open source VPN tunnel,

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:40.860
to connect the
Virginia data centers.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:42.990
Once the traffic
was inside Google,

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:46.860
it crossed the Atlantic on
Google's fast internal network

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:49.920
instead of over
the open internet.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:54.180
And we were able to get the
performance that we needed.

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:57.870
The final thing that we
feared were live migrations.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:59.880
And we really feared them.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:02.130
Live migrations are
actually very clever.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.680
When Google needs to
replace hardware or update

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:08.640
their hypervisor, they move your
VM from one piece of hardware

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:10.500
to another behind the scene.

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:13.750
When we first heard about this,
we thought it was really cool.

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:17.380
But then we were afraid of what
it was going to do to HBase.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:21.660
So with HBase, we keep a strict
6 millisecond response time.

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:24.480
And this is important because
the HubSpot UI touches

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.510
many different services,
and those services touch

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:29.170
many other services.

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:32.730
So any delay in any request
can cause a cascading effect,

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:35.562
resulting in a slow
UI for our users.

00:17:35.562 --> 00:17:37.770
So even though the difference
between a 6 millisecond

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:40.650
response time and a 10
millisecond response time

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:42.480
doesn't seem like
much, that makes

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.550
a difference between a slow and
snappy UI for our customers.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.890
So the way live
migrations work is

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:53.380
Google takes a
snapshot of your VM

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:56.740
and starts an identical
copy on other hardware.

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.350
Then they pause the
original VM, copy over

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:02.500
anything that changed, and
then start up a new VM.

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:05.590
The Google folks told us that
this could take up to a second,

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:08.530
depending on how much RAM
was on the original VM

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:11.500
or how heavy the RAM churn was.

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:15.040
HBase is very right-intensive,
meaning the pause time

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:17.110
could end up being very long.

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:21.320
And that could be catastrophic
to the response time.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:24.770
So to solve this, we spun up
an HBase cluster in Google.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:28.220
We replayed production
workloads through that cluster,

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:32.780
had the Google engineers force
a bunch of live migrations,

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:36.270
and we watched our graphs
to see the effects.

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:39.530
We were shocked to see there
were no effects at all.

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:41.480
So at that point, our
three fears were gone.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:46.140
So we committed to moving
forward with Google in the EU.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:50.470
So now that our fears were
gone, what was the plan?

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:52.040
This was four steps.

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:53.420
Update Rainmaker.

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:55.790
Spin up the platform components.

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:58.200
Work out all the application
dependency issues.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:03.270
And finally update our DNS
to send traffic to the EU.

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:05.150
So it took us five
days to update--

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:10.730
it took us five days to
set up our infrastructure

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:11.870
to deploy to Frankfurt.

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:14.030
We had to move from
Belgium to Frankfurt.

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:17.530
This involved setting up DNS
services, as well as Puppet.

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:20.330
Rainmaker was also really easy
to set up because we already

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:21.530
had the adapter for Google.

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:23.390
So it was simply
pointing to a new region.

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:27.610
Next was the bulk
of the work, where

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:31.060
we considered HubSpot really
multi-cloud and multi-region.

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:32.890
Our team spun up the
clusters we needed

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:36.800
to run our platform in Google,
including ZooKeeper, HBase,

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.620
LDAP, the Vault.
We had originally

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:43.680
hacked a deploy
option to Belgium

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:46.080
early on to get a proof
of concept for Orion

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:48.700
to deploy to Google in the EU.

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:51.260
So we rewrote it the right
way and deployed analytics

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:53.220
as our first test service.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:56.070
We also got the product
engineering teams excited

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:57.660
and showed them
the simple way they

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:01.560
could configure their services
to run in both regions or just

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:03.430
one or the other.

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:05.880
So they started deploying
at that time too.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:07.680
This took us 30
days to get right.

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:13.180
The product teams started
untangling their application

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:14.686
services at that time.

00:20:14.686 --> 00:20:16.060
They figured out
what they needed

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:20.500
to run to support our data
collection services in the EU

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:22.900
independently, without
reaching back to the US.

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:25.000
We had quite a few
to work through

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.730
at that time and plenty of
skeletons in the closet.

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.250
Apart from spinning up clusters
and deploying services,

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:36.100
there was actually an
even bigger hurdle.

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:40.660
And this was the human
element of the project.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:44.650
We have more than 60 small,
autonomous engineering teams.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:47.860
They are in Dublin, Boston,
as well as remote teams.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:49.900
With so many
microservices, we needed

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:52.030
to figure out the
dependencies and uncover

00:20:52.030 --> 00:20:54.355
all of those skeletons
as fast as possible.

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:58.360
So this was the key question.

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:03.460
How do you get everyone to
find all of the skeletons?

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:06.430
So the way we did this was
with a month and a half

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:08.170
of weekly chaos testing.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:10.510
We decided this was the
best way to get everyone

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:13.810
on the same page, to figure
out what we didn't know about,

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:17.300
and to move fast together.

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:20.060
So we got an engineer
from each product team

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:22.550
who owned a collection service,
along with the platform

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:23.870
engineers.

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:25.730
We bought them all lunch.

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:27.350
We dialed in the Dublin team.

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:28.730
And we broke the network.

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:33.760
So that first test, they
were all very confident

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:35.890
that everything
was going to work.

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:38.530
But there was a huge list
of things that broke.

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:40.480
Our goal was to cut
the network and be

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:42.650
able to restart all
of those services

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:44.920
and ensure that they
could survive completely

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:46.360
independently.

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:47.600
So we made a list.

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:50.890
And it was a very long list
of everything that had broken

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:53.300
and all of the missed
dependencies-- for example,

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:56.050
databases that were still
running in the US that needed

00:21:56.050 --> 00:21:59.830
to be replicated, authentication
services, domain services

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:01.010
as well.

00:22:01.010 --> 00:22:03.190
So we added those
teams to future tests.

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:06.490
And the list of what we
needed to fix was long.

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:08.710
But the teams all agreed
to fix them and come back

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:10.330
the next week.

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:13.360
So we did this each
week for six weeks.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:15.940
And each week, the list
got shorter and shorter,

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:18.700
until there was no more list.

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:20.830
So we even came up
with some creative ways

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.140
to cause failures between
the two data centers

00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:26.610
and tried those as well.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.530
Until finally, it was all green.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:30.030
And we were confident
we could run

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:35.190
in the EU data center completely
independent from the US.

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:38.820
The final step was to enable
Cloudflare [? GOD-based ?] DNS

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:40.560
for our production traffic.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.860
That took a few days to roll
out because we did each service

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:45.780
one at a time to
make sure that there

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:48.220
were no disruptions
to our customers.

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:50.715
So the overall time line
was, in fact, longer than

00:22:50.715 --> 00:22:54.300
the 30 days I promised in
the title of this talk.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:57.540
But we were officially
multi-cloud and multi-region

00:22:57.540 --> 00:23:00.690
with our platform
and one test service,

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:03.760
but just not fully
production-ready, in 30 days.

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:06.750
But a short seven weeks later,
we met our deadline with room

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:08.160
to spare.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:09.180
So what was the result?

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:17.020
Today 9% of our VMs are
running in Google Cloud.

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:21.070
We saw a 5x speed-up in response
time for our European customers

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.530
due to SSL
terminating in Europe.

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:25.780
And 17% of our
collection requests

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:28.330
now are processed
on Google Cloud.

00:23:28.330 --> 00:23:31.240
The EU was also spared
two service disruptions

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.490
when there were
network issues as well.

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.680
One funny thing that
happened during our testing

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:39.080
was a trans-Pacific network
cut during our tests.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:42.130
So requests from Australia
were getting routed

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:44.320
to Europe instead of the US.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.390
So we were getting a
similar unexpected boost

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:50.650
for our customers in
Australia at the same time.

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:52.500
So at the end of
Q1, we celebrated.

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:57.880
And the team has moved on
to their next big mission

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:00.460
after some great lessons
learned about going

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:05.120
multi-region and multi-cloud
at the same time.

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:08.670
So let's recap
those key takeaways.

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:12.900
List and test your fears
first and do this fast.

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:13.800
Keep it simple.

00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:16.350
Do not bite off more than you
can chew with the first go.

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:19.810
There will be
plenty of unknowns.

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:22.420
Use chaos testing
as your best friend.

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:25.630
It is a great forcing function
to uncover those skeletons

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:28.970
and shed light on
what is not done.

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:31.040
Treat your
infrastructure as code.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:35.980
Use abstractions to be able
to experiment and move fast.

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:37.870
And finally, don't
forget to use lunch

00:24:37.870 --> 00:24:41.070
to coerce the human
side of the project.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.650
Thank you.

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:46.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

